Description: pvr: lay out varyings by component
 Vertex outputs and fragment inputs get one range per location
 (try_allocate_vars() in pvr_arch_pipeline.c), and the shaders address
 component c of a location at range start + c (VS store_output) or
 range start + 4 * c (FS coefficients, pco_trans_nir.c). The range size was
 the sum of the dword slots of all variables at the location, which equals
 the highest used component only if the variables start at component 0 and
 have no gaps. nir_compact_varyings() leaves variables with always_active_io
 in place (nir_linking_helpers.c), which spirv_to_nir sets for transform
 feedback outputs, so a fragment shader that reads only the upper components
 of a captured output keeps a variable at component 2 or 3. Its range then
 is too small, the store or coefficient load of that component lands in the
 range of the next location, and the coefficient program copies the wrong
 components. The coefficient setup only reported the differing sizes with
 pvr_finishme() ("amend nir var linking").
 .
 Size each range up to the highest component used at the location, so the
 component offsets the shaders use stay inside it. Count the per-mode
 varying dwords for TA_STATE_VARYING0/1 from the allocated ranges instead
 of the variable components, so the TA sees the same layout. The coefficient
 program iterates the components both stages provide; components the vertex
 shader does not write stay undefined, as the specification allows. Linked
 pipelines without gaps get the same layout and counts as before.
 .
 Only for this downstream stack; not intended for upstream submission.
